````markdown
# Host attractors: where a lararium can live, and what each host gets to be

Fable web researcher, tasked spirit, 2026-10-16. Inputs read in full: the boot seed (`noosphere-boot.mem`), `portable-engine-plan-v2.md`, `model-and-gaps.md`. `third-ways.md` was left alone (still in progress). No repository code was read or run; the "what the branch gives" column rests on the survey's citations. Nothing in the tree was touched.

**Reach in this pass.** `WebSearch` worked in both modes. `WebFetch` resolved only `github.com`; `developers.cloudflare.com` failed DNS (`getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND`), and I did not retry other non-GitHub hosts given the sibling's identical finding. `gh search code` and `gh api repos/Mudlet/Mudlet` were refused: "sessions are bound to their configured repositories" / "GitHub access to this repository is not enabled for this session". Marks: **[reached: fetched]** page read (GitHub only) · **[reached: search]** confirmed through search-returned passages · **[unreached]** memory only, flagged where used. Inferences read *(inferred)*.

**Vocabulary used throughout.** *Store* = the CRDT (conflict-free replicated data type: a data structure that merges concurrent edits without a coordinator), one Automerge document per bag, the virtual backend. *Engine* = the headless TW5 VM: parser, filters, widgets and actions, wiki state, no DOM. *Projection* = any render or interaction surface: a DOM, a 3D scene, a Lua text pane, a terminal, a chat message, an MCP tool result. *Port* = the narrow protocol between engine and projection (the Tiddler Port of v2). *Host* = the thing that executes JavaScript (or WASM) and offers storage, networking and threads. *Lararium* = one operator's node: store + engine + at least one projection.

---

## 1. Summary

1. **The frame holds against the field.** Every host surveyed falls cleanly into one of four coupling tiers once STORE, ENGINE and PROJECTION are separated: **(a)** full lararium inside; **(b)** engine inside, store synced elsewhere; **(c)** projection only, coupled to a nearby lararium over a port; **(d)** not viable. No host needed a fifth category. The one axis the field adds is *whether the host can run WebAssembly*, because Automerge is Rust compiled to WASM with no pure-JS build [reached: search; automerge-anywhere blog], while Hermes has no `WebAssembly` global (issue open since 2020, maintainer recommends transpiling to asm.js instead) [reached: fetched] and QuickJS has none either [reached: search]. So a JS engine without WASM can carry the ENGINE but not the STORE unless the host supplies Automerge natively.

2. **The minimal engine-host contract is small**: an ES2020 engine, `new Function` (the kernel evaluates the core blob that way), a microtask queue, a timer or a host-driven pump, a message channel, a monotonic clock, 64-bit-ish memory budget in the tens of MB, no DOM, no Node, no threads assumed. Extism's JS PDK (QuickJS-ng in WASM, ES2020, no `setTimeout`, no event loop) [reached: fetched] marks the floor below which TW5's own refresh throttles stop working; mikrojs on ESP32 (80–250 KB heap) [reached: search] marks the floor below which the engine does not fit at all.

3. **The STORE already has the right abstraction**: automerge-repo takes exactly one storage adapter (any key/value store with range queries) and many network adapters (WebSocket client/server, MessageChannel, BroadcastChannel, IndexedDB, NodeFS shipped) [reached: fetched; reached: search]. Portability of the store across hosts is a matter of writing adapters over whatever durable thing the host offers, and of refusing by name where the host offers nothing durable (Tauri's WebView storage does not survive app updates per PowerSync; iOS can evict WebView IndexedDB per Capacitor's own guide) [reached: search].

4. **The PROJECTION port must carry one more thing than v2's ten kinds**: a *render ask* that returns a neutral intermediate (plain text, HTML string, or a small block tree), because a Lua pane, a Verse scene, a Discord message and an MCP resource cannot run wikitext themselves. With that, one engine serves a DOM (stock TW5 as replica camera), a 3D world, a Lua pane and an MCP tool. Prior art for one runtime behind many surfaces is abundant and consistent: ink's C# core with ports to JS, C++, Dart and plugins for Unity, Unreal 5.3+, Godot, where "the same story and seed produce the same output and saves" [reached: search]; Yarn Spinner's "generic dialogue virtual machine… with nothing Unity-specific in it" now ported to GDScript and being built natively for Unreal [reached: search]; MCP resources with URIs, `subscribe` and `notifications/resources/updated` [reached: fetched].

4b. **The house's two-grain hypothesis holds with one correction** (§2.4): TW5's widget tree already carries affordances declaratively (`$link to`, `$button actions/message`, `$checkbox`, `$select`, `$edit-text`), so one parse yields both an HTML render for a rich glass and a blocks-plus-affordances stream for lean hosts, invoked by handle under a render cursor. But there are two *read* grains and only one *write* grain: records with `txId` stay the sole state carrier; edits on lean hosts go through `port:write`, never through repeated invokes.

5. **Unreal Engine 6** (announced at State of Unreal / Unreal Fest, June 2026; early access "late 2027-ish", full release 12–18 months later) standardizes gameplay on Verse with a Scene Graph replacing Actors; Verse runs single-threaded with transactional semantics that extend to C++ it calls; Verse today exists only inside UEFN; nothing public says the compiler will be open-sourced or that UE6 embeds a JS engine [reached: search, several]. Verdict: **tier (c) sidecar now and for UE6**, with UE5.x able to reach **(b)** through PuerTS (V8 / QuickJS / Node backends, "4.22 ~ latest") [reached: fetched]. **Mudlet** embeds Lua 5.1, speaks GMCP and friends over telnet, has HTTP but no native WebSocket, and offers Geyser panes plus a new embeddable `Geyser.TextEdit` [reached: search]; verdict: **tier (c)**, with the lararium posing as the "game server" on localhost and GMCP as the port.

6. **Ranked forward attractors**: make first-class next (1) browsers and WebView shells, (2) Node/Deno/Bun including Pi and Termux, (3) VS Code and Obsidian (their extension-host + webview split *is* the port), (4) an MCP projection, (5) a Foundry VTT module; projection-only (6) Mudlet over GMCP, (7) Owlbear Rodeo, (8) UE6 sidecar, (9) Neovim/Emacs; engine-inside-when-asked (10) Godot/Unity/UE5 via GodotJS/PuerTS; relay-only behind the island boundary (11) Cloudflare Durable Objects; drop as engine hosts: Roll20 (sandbox forbids HTTP and external code), e-readers (basic JS only; static camera at most), ESP32-class (heap), Bevy (no JS scripting at all).

7. **What the branch already gives**: the third-runtime forge that nulls both platform flags, the `require` allowlist and `fs`-denying proxy, the CID integrity gate, Automerge-per-bag with Repo sync over MessageChannel and a WebSocket relay adapter, a Node `worker_thread` path with a disk projector, the fakedom static camera, and the island envelope family. Missing for the wider field: a generic `filter`/`render` ask on the live island (the one such surface, `meme-worker-script.ts`, is orphaned per the survey), and the Node path's asymmetries (survey gap #10).

---

## 2. The three-layer contract

### 2.1 What the ENGINE may assume of a host (minimum)

Drawn from the floor and ceiling hosts found in the field; each line names the evidence.

| Assumption | Why this and not more | Evidence |
|---|---|---|
| **ES2020 language level**, no reliance on later syntax in the core blob | QuickJS targets ES2020 (Extism/Javy builds) and "almost complete ES2023" upstream; GodotJS claims "near-complete ES2020"; Hermes lags on some features *(inferred: Hermes historically omitted `with`, some RegExp features, and local `eval`; unverified here)* | [reached: fetched] Extism PDK; [reached: search] QuickJS, GodotJS |
| **`new Function` / indirect eval available** | The kernel evaluates the core with `new Function("exports","module","require","window","process", src)` (survey §3.1). Hermes supports eval without JIT *(inferred)*; Extism's PDK runs Wizer snapshots, where runtime eval is possible but un-idiomatic | survey; [reached: fetched] Extism |
| **A microtask queue (Promises)** | Universal across hosts surveyed, including Extism (Promise present) | [reached: fetched] |
| **A timer, or a host pump** | TW5's draft throttle (`setTimeout`, 400 ms), the HandGate (400 ms trailing / 2 s cap) and the CoalesceGate all rest on timers. Extism's PDK has **no `setTimeout`, no event loop**, `queueMicrotask` runs immediately. So the contract names a `host.schedule(ms, fn)` capability; where absent, the engine runs in **pump mode**: the host calls `engine.tick(nowMs)` and the engine drains its own timer wheel *(inferred design)* | [reached: fetched] Extism; survey |
| **A message channel** | postMessage-shaped (browser Worker, Node `worker_threads`, VS Code webview JSON messages, Owlbear iframe), or host-function-shaped (Extism `Host.inputString/outputString`, PuerTS/GodotJS bindings). Payloads must be **JSON-serializable** at minimum (VS Code refuses Blob/File/ImageData; older versions dropped ArrayBuffers) | [reached: search] VS Code; [reached: fetched] Extism |
| **A monotonic clock** | `performance.now` exists even in Extism | [reached: fetched] |
| **No DOM, no Node globals, no `window`** | The third runtime already nulls `$tw.browser` and `$tw.node` (survey §3.1); Roll20 and Extism prove "JS without a browser or Node" is a real class of host | survey; [reached: search] Roll20 |
| **No threads assumed; threads used when offered** | Godot's workers are "limited support (experimental)"; Cloudflare Workers have no worker threads; Extism has none; browsers/Node/Deno/Bun have them | [reached: fetched] GodotJS; [reached: search] Deno |
| **Memory in the tens of MB** | Node TW ≈ 33–70 MB resident (v2 §0.10); mikrojs on ESP32 offers 80–250 KB; Espruino ≈ 33 KB of JS space. Below ~16 MB the engine is a projection leaf, not an engine | v2; [reached: search] |
| **No WASM assumed by the engine itself** | The engine is plain JS; only the STORE wants WASM. This keeps the engine runnable on Hermes and QuickJS | [reached: fetched] Hermes #429 |

Everything else (filesystem, network, IndexedDB, WebSocket) is a **capability the host injects** (v2 principle P7), never assumed. The conformance suite of v2 Phase 3 proves the contract per host; host detection never does.

### 2.2 How the STORE abstracts storage and sync

- **Shape already in hand.** automerge-repo: "A repo accepts only one storage adapter, and it must be set when the repo is created. It can have several network adapters, which can be added after creation"; custom storage is "any key/value store that supports range queries"; shipped: `storage-indexeddb`, `storage-nodefs`, `network-websocket` (client and server), `network-messagechannel`, `network-broadcastchannel` [reached: fetched; reached: search]. Tabs sharing IndexedDB "don't see live updates on their own" and need a channel adapter beside the storage adapter [reached: search], which is v2's P15 topology.
- **The WASM line.** Automerge ships WASM with a base64-embedded fallback "as the ultimate fallback", still WASM [reached: search]. Hermes: no `WebAssembly` global; maintainer (Sept 2024): "the recommended approach is transpiling Wasm to Asm.js" [reached: fetched]. QuickJS/quickjs-ng: no built-in WASM; it can itself be *compiled to* WASM, which is the opposite direction [reached: search]. React Native 0.84 (Feb 2026) made Hermes V1 default; the official post says nothing about WASM, though one secondary summary claimed it [reached: search, conflicting]. Consequence: **on Hermes and QuickJS the store cannot be Automerge-in-JS.** Two honest options: (i) the host runs Automerge natively and exposes it through host functions (Automerge has Rust at its core; Swift and Kotlin bindings exist [unreached, memory]); (ii) the host is tier (b): engine inside, store on a neighbor reached over a network adapter. Yjs runs as pure JavaScript (~18 kB) [reached: search], but the house chose Automerge and the operator's own TW5-Yjs showed its grain; switching stores for one host class would split the Nexus, so option (i)/(ii) stands.
- **Durability is a per-host claim, refused by name when false.** Tauri WebView: IndexedDB and OPFS "don't persist across app updates" (PowerSync, vendor claim) [reached: search]. Capacitor on iOS: the OS can reclaim WebView storage "and the same can be said for IndexedDB" [reached: search]. SQLite-WASM over OPFS needs a Worker and COOP/COEP headers [reached: search]. Cloudflare Durable Objects: SQLite backend ~10 GB per object (GA April 2025), hibernation drops memory but keeps WebSockets [reached: search; official limits page unreached]. Roll20: no storage beyond its `state` object and no HTTP [reached: search]. Owlbear: room metadata "must be under 16kB", broadcast payload ≤ 16 KB [reached: search]. So the store adapter table carries a `durable: boolean` and a `budget` per host; a non-durable host must sync to a durable neighbor before it may call itself a lararium. This is the seed's causal-island law in storage terms: a node knows only *as of my last sync*.
- **Network adapters per port kind.** WebSocket (browser, Node, UE's `IWebSocket`, Godot/Unity sockets, Foundry's socket.io `module.<id>` channel), MessageChannel (worker, VS Code webview, Owlbear iframe via postMessage), stdio/JSON-RPC (MCP, Neovim msgpack-RPC, Emacs `jsonrpc.el`), telnet+GMCP (Mudlet, Evennia). Only the first three carry CRDT sync; GMCP carries projection traffic, never the store *(inferred: GMCP payloads are JSON over telnet subnegotiation and could carry Automerge sync bytes base64'd, but no host wants that)*.

### 2.3 What a PROJECTION port must carry

v2's ten kinds stand (`open`, `write`, `ask`, `presence`, `close` up; `snapshot`, `changes`, `written`, `answer`, `presence`, `fault` down). The multi-host field asks for three refinements, none of which reintroduces the rejected syncer or a DOM-delta stream:

1. **A render ask with a declared target.** `port:ask{kind:"render", title, target:"text"|"html"|"blocks"}`. A DOM projection never needs it (the glass TW5 renders itself). A Lua pane needs `text` (or Mudlet's own tiny HTML subset for labels), a Discord bot needs `text`/markdown, an MCP resource needs `text/markdown` or `text/html`, a 3D world needs `blocks` (a small tree: heading, paragraph, list, link, field) to map onto meshes or widgets. The branch's fakedom camera already renders RootTemplate to HTML in the worker (survey §3.4); `text` and `blocks` are serializers over the same fake tree *(inferred)*. Yarn Spinner and ink both hand the host *lines and options*, not a UI, and let each engine draw them [reached: search]; this ask is the same move.
2. **Acts arrive as writes or verbs, never as events.** A Geyser label click, a Verse interaction, an MCP tool call, a Discord slash command each become `port:write{records}` or `port:ask{kind:"verb"}` with an actor. This honours the operator's rejection of "the mutators": the projection owns its own input semantics and the engine never sees a DOM event.
3. **A capability manifest at `port:open`.** The projection declares what it can show and do: `renders: ["html"]`, `edits: true|false`, `presence: true|false`, `maxRecordBytes`, `maxBatchBytes` (Owlbear's 16 KB, Discord's 2 000-character message, Mudlet's ~10k HTTP body cap [reached: search] all matter). The engine then chunks and skinnies to fit, and refuses by name what it cannot fit (v2 P8).

A projection, by this contract, is *a replica that may be lossy in rendering but never in state*: it may show a paragraph as plain text, but what it writes back is a whole tiddler with a `txId` and a `basis`.

### 2.4 The two-grain hypothesis (from the house's coffee talk), weighed

**The hypothesis.** The engine↔projection waist may need two grains: *rich* projections (a DOM glass) host a TW5 replica and render from records; *lean* projections (Mudlet, chat, MCP, a UE dialogue panel) receive an **affordance stream**: content, plus the actions offered on it, plus an invoke, in the shape of ink's lines-plus-choices runtime. Open question: does TW5's own parse/widget tree already carry the affordances, so one source feeds both grains?

**Where it holds.**

1. *The widget tree does carry affordances, declaratively.* TW5's core widgets name their acts as attributes, not as event handlers: `$link to=…`, `$button` with `actions`, `message`/`param`, `set`/`setTo`, `popup`; `$checkbox` with `tiddler`/`field`/`checked`/`unchecked`; `$select` with its options; `$edit-text` with `tiddler`/`field`; `$droppable`/`$draggable` with `actions`. The branch's own probes confirm the tree is walkable headless: the fakedom camera renders the full widget tree in the worker and the `addEventListener` patch already stamps a per-node handle (`data-lar-rid`) wherever a widget bound a listener (survey §3.4). So an *affordance serializer* over the same fake tree, emitting `{id, kind: link|button|toggle|select|edit|drop, label, target|message|field}` per node, needs no second source. One parse, two serializations: HTML for the glass, blocks-plus-affordances for the lean host. The hypothesis refines my `blocks` render target from passive to invokable, and I adopt that refinement: **`blocks` carries `affordances[]`.**
2. *Invoke maps onto `port:ask{kind:"invoke"}`, not onto an event.* A lean host sends `{renderCursor, affordanceId, arg?}`; the engine finds the live widget and calls its own `invokeActions`/`dispatchEvent(tm-*)` with the widget's variable context. This stays on the right side of the operator's ruling: no DOM event crosses, no mutation log; a named act on a named affordance, answered by name. The branch's `ridMap → TW5's stored handler` path (survey §3.3) is already this call, minus the cursor.
3. *Ink's grain is the right precedent for the lifetime rule.* In ink a choice list is valid until the next `Continue`; in TW5 an affordance list is valid until the next refresh that touched that subtree. So an invoke carries the `renderCursor` it was offered under, and a stale invoke answers `stale-target` (the branch already has this answer for rids, survey §3.3). This turns the branch's unbounded rid growth (survey gap #6) into a bounded, cursored lease: affordance ids live one render, and the engine prunes at flush.
4. *Host evidence fits lean hosts exactly.* Discord message components carry a `custom_id` round-tripped on click [unreached, memory]; MCP tools can be listed dynamically with `tools/list_changed`, so the current view's affordances can become the current tool list *(inferred from the spec's capability flags)* [reached: fetched, resources page only]; Mudlet labels take `echoLink`/right-click menus that call a Lua function with an id; a UE dialogue panel is literally a lines-plus-choices widget. None of these wants HTML; all of them want content + choices + an id to send back.
5. *Rich projections stay record-driven.* The glass TW5 never needs the affordance stream because it runs the widgets itself; grain 1 (records with cursors) is sufficient, and v2's whole argument for it stands untouched.

**Where it breaks, or bends.**

1. *Edit is not a choice.* `$edit-text`, drag-reorder, keyboard shortcuts and multi-step dialogs are continuous, not one-shot. The affordance stream can *announce* them (`kind: "edit", tiddler, field`), but their fulfilment has to go through grain 1 (`port:write` of a whole record with `txId`) or a dedicated verb, never through repeated invokes. Mudlet's new `Geyser.TextEdit` and MCP's `write`-style tools fit that split; a UE dialogue panel does not edit at all. So the two grains are not alternatives: **a lean projection reads grain 2 and writes grain 1.**
2. *Action semantics live in the widget's context.* A `$button actions=…` string is wikitext evaluated against the widget's variables (`currentTiddler`, list items, transclusion params). Only the live engine widget can evaluate it; a lean host must never be handed the action string to run itself. Hence invoke-by-handle, and hence the engine must keep the rendered widget tree resident per open lean port, which costs memory per projection (survey gap #6 priced one tree at 11.7 KB HTML per frame plus rids; a per-port tree multiplies that). Skinny snapshots do not help here; render-on-demand per visible tiddler does.
3. *Affordances are state-dependent and so is their legality.* Popups, `$reveal` and `$list` make the affordance set a function of `$:/state/*`, which the tier filter keeps *on the glass* (v2 P10). For a lean port the engine must hold that session state itself, per port. That is fine (the engine already holds `$:/state` for the fakedom camera), but it means a lean port is a session, not a stateless reader, and `port:close` must clear it.
4. *Authority does not come from the tree.* An affordance being offered says the widget would fire it; it does not say the actor may. Invoke still passes the capability gate (`requestedBy`, keyhive) before `invokeActions`, else the lean path becomes a bypass of the kind survey gap #16 names.
5. *Not every TW5 act is a widget.* Root `tm-*` services (copy, download, print, open-window) are not affordances of a tiddler; they are host services, and on a lean host most are refused by name (survey §5.3). The affordance stream should not pretend to carry them.
6. *Mixed-grain hosts exist.* Obsidian, VS Code and Foundry can host a full replica *and* want a lean summary (a sidebar, a tree view, a tooltip). Two grains from one engine serve this well; two grains from two engines (a glass TW5 plus a worker) would double the affordance state. Prefer the lean stream to be served by whichever engine holds the port, and never by the glass replica.

**Verdict.** The hypothesis holds, with one correction: it names *two read grains*, but there is only *one write grain*. Records with cursors (grain 1) remain the sole state carrier both ways; the affordance stream (grain 2) is a second *read* serialization of the engine's live widget tree, cursored per render, invoked by handle through the capability gate, and never a path for state to change except by calling back into the engine. TW5's widget tree already carries what grain 2 needs; the serializer, the per-port session, and the cursored invoke are the work. This folds into §2.3's render ask as `target: "blocks"` with `affordances[]` and a new `port:ask{kind:"invoke"}`, and into §9.2's sketch.

---

## 3. Host families

Columns: JS/WASM execution today · storage and network for the store · threads · natural projection · tier. "WASM" marks whether Automerge-in-JS can load.

### 3.1 Browsers and WebView shells

| Host | Execution | Storage / network | Threads | Projection | Tier |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Desktop and mobile browsers | V8/JSC/SpiderMonkey, full ES, WASM | IndexedDB, OPFS (in a Worker), WebSocket, WebRTC | Workers, SharedWorker (Safari 16+), Web Locks (v2 P15) | DOM (stock TW5 as replica camera) | **(a)** |
| Capacitor / Tauri / Electron WebView | Same engines; Electron adds Node | **Caveat:** Tauri WebView IndexedDB/OPFS wiped on app update (vendor claim); iOS may evict WebView storage; Electron has Node FS | Workers; Electron adds `worker_threads` and `utilityProcess` | DOM | **(a)** with the storage adapter moved to a native FS/SQLite plugin (Capacitor Filesystem routes to Swift/Kotlin natively) [reached: search] |
| Smart TV (Tizen 8–10 on Chromium M108–M130; webOS TV 22 on Chromium 87, older on 68–79) | Chromium of its year; IndexedDB listed on Tizen (table garbled); Service Workers unsupported on Tizen | IndexedDB (Tizen); WebSocket | Workers | DOM, remote-driven | Tizen 8+ **(a)** feasible; webOS ≤ 22 **(c)** or lean (a) *(inferred from Chromium age)* [reached: search] |
| E-readers (Kindle/Kobo experimental browsers) | "basic JavaScript and CSS but little else"; Kindle build lacked Promises/fetch (2019 report); Kobo on WebKit 533 (2012) | none dependable | none | Static HTML from the fakedom camera | **(d)** as engine; **(c-static)** as a read-only camera [reached: search] |
| WebXR headsets (Meta Quest Browser, Chromium ~130–136) | Chromium, WASM, WebXR | IndexedDB, WebSocket | Workers | DOM + WebXR scene (three.js/A-Frame as a `blocks` projection) | **(a)** [reached: search] |

### 3.2 JS runtimes

| Host | Execution | Storage / network | Threads | Projection | Tier |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Node.js (incl. Raspberry Pi arm64, Termux aarch64 where Node 24 is reported running) | V8, WASM | FS (`storage-nodefs`, multi-process safe), WebSocket server/client, UDS | `worker_threads` (the branch's node island) | none native; disk projector, TUI, HTTP | **(a)** [reached: fetched; reached: search for Termux single report] |
| Deno | V8, WASM; module-type Workers only; per-worker `deno.permissions` | FS with permission flags; WebSocket | Workers | same as Node | **(a)** [reached: search] |
| Bun | JSC, WASM; `bun:sqlite` | FS, SQLite, WebSocket | Workers *(inferred: Bun docs unreached)* | same as Node | **(a)** |
| Cloudflare Workers / Durable Objects | V8 isolates, WASM | DO SQLite (~10 GB/object), no FS; WebSockets with hibernation (memory dropped, sockets kept) | none | HTTP/WS relay only | **(a)** technically (engine reboots on each wake), but better **relay-only** for the store behind the island boundary; a web2 cloud sits outside the stack proper [reached: search] |

### 3.3 Embedded JS engines

| Host | Execution | Storage / network | Threads | Projection | Tier |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| QuickJS / quickjs-ng (C embed) | ES2020–ES2023, **no WASM**, boots in <300 µs | whatever the C host lends | none built in | host-defined | **(b)**, or **(a)** only if the host lends native Automerge through host functions [reached: search] |
| Hermes (React Native) | ES subset, bytecode AOT, no JIT, **no WASM** (issue #429 open) | RN modules: SQLite, FS; WebSocket | JSI threads via native modules | React Native views (a `blocks` projection) | **(b)**; **(a)** only with a native Automerge module [reached: fetched] |
| JavaScriptCore (Apple, Bun, GodotJS backend) | full ES, WASM on Apple platforms *(inferred)* | host-lent | host-lent | host-defined | **(a)** possible |
| Javy / Extism JS PDK (QuickJS-ng in WASM under wasmtime/WASI) | ES2020, synchronous, **no timers, no event loop, no Worker, no WebSocket, no dynamic import**; `Var` key/value persists only for the plugin's lifetime; host functions take ≤5 i64 args | none durable inside; host HTTP via `fetch` | none | pure function: render/filter answers | **(b-pure)**: engine as a stateless function over a snapshot the host passes in; never a live wiki *(inferred)* [reached: fetched] |
| ESP32 (Espruino ≈ 33 KB JS space; mikrojs/QuickJS-ng 80–250 KB heap) | tiny JS | flash, Wi-Fi/MQTT | none | a lamp, a gauge, a sensor | **(d)** as engine; **(c-leaf)**: reads one record over WS/MQTT, writes one back [reached: search] |

