## 4. Deep dive: Unreal Engine 6 and Verse

**What is public as of October 2026** (all [reached: search] unless marked):

- Epic unveiled UE6 at State of Unreal / Unreal Fest 2026 (Inven Global dates the keynote to "the 17th" in Chicago; 4Gamer's keynote report is dated 2026-06-19). Early access is targeted for "late 2027-ish", full release 12–18 months after; the next Rocket League is named as a launch title. Epic's own "The Road to UE 6" post is the primary source (unrealengine.com unreached; quoted through forums and GamesBeat).
- Sweeney's first of three shifts: "standardizing gameplay logic on Verse and building a unified engine API set", so gameplay objects and subsystems move between projects. UE6 merges the UE5 track and the UEFN track.
- Verse underpins a **Scene Graph** that replaces the Actor system; Blueprints and Actors remain "in early versions of UE6" and are deprecated "when the new framework is sufficiently mature". GamesBeat frames Verse as the gameplay layer "alongside the low level C engine"; the Ludus vendor blog and Inven say "replace C++". The careful reading: C++ stays underneath and callable.
- Runtime shape quoted from Epic's text via the forum: "Verse runs on a single thread and calls C++ code built via a custom LLVM compiler that automatically transactionalizes the C++ code"; "transactional semantics also extend to any C++ code that is called from Verse".
- Verse is "currently tied to Fortnite" (UEFN) and "not intended for general adoption at this point". No result mentions open-sourcing the Verse compiler, embedding a JavaScript engine, or WebAssembly in UE6. Epic's older statements about publishing the Verse language spec remain [unreached, memory].

**Embedding JS in Unreal today.** PuerTS supports "unreal engine 4.22 ~ latest" with three backends: V8 (recommended; "ECMAScript only, with no Node.js or browser APIs"), QuickJS (smallest, "no debugging support"), Node.js (full Node APIs, largest, OpenSSL disabled on mobile) [reached: fetched]. Unreal.js is effectively frozen at UE 5.0–5.1 (last push July 2023) [reached: search]. Unreal's built-in networking offers `IWebSocket` (client, via `IWebSocketsManager::CreateWebSocket`), an experimental `WebSocketNetworking` plugin (client and server), and an experimental `WebSocketMessaging` transport [reached: search]. The built-in Web Browser widget rests on an old CEF (a community wiki says CEF 59); several Fab plugins ship newer CEF with a JS↔UE bridge [reached: search].

**Reading for the lararium.**

1. **UE5.x, tier (b) today.** A PuerTS V8 or QuickJS backend can run the ENGINE inside the game process (ES2020 satisfied; the kernel's `new Function` path works on V8; QuickJS needs the pump mode for timers if PuerTS's timer shim proves thin *(inferred)*). The STORE needs WASM (V8 has it, but PuerTS exposes "ECMAScript only", so Automerge's WASM must load through a C++ host function that returns bytes *(inferred)*) or a native Automerge in C++ lent through the binding. The pragmatic shape: engine inside, store on a sidecar or on the desktop Node backend, UMG/3D as a `blocks` projection. Unreal.js is not a path forward.
2. **UE6, tier (c) sidecar, by design not by lack.** Verse's single thread with transactional semantics is the wrong place for a long-running wiki VM: a transaction that rolls back would need the engine's state to roll back with it *(inferred from the quoted semantics)*. The lararium therefore runs beside the game (a Node or QuickJS process, or the house's node island) and the game holds a **port** over `IWebSocket` or a C++ pipe. Scene Graph entities become projections: a `blocks` render of a tiddler becomes a sign, a book, a shrine panel; interactions become `port:write`s with the player as actor. This is also what the operator's "tightly coupled" allows: coupling at the port, never at the thread.
3. **What to watch.** (i) Whether UE6 ships a scripting bridge from Verse to anything other than C++; (ii) whether the Web Browser widget moves to a current CEF (a current CEF would make the stock glass TW5 a first-class projection inside UE); (iii) whether PuerTS tracks UE6's C++ surface. None of these is blocked by Epic's statements; none is promised.

A deliberately flagged uncertainty: the whole UE6 picture rests on keynote coverage, forum quotations of Epic's post, and one vendor blog with a commercial interest. The operator can take it to contact by reading "The Road to UE 6" directly.

---

## 5. Deep dive: Mudlet

**Facts** (all [reached: search]; the GitHub README added only that Lua, Qt, GMCP, Discord integration, LuaSQL, LuaFileSystem, lua-yajl and SQLite are in the credits [reached: fetched]):

- Scripting: **Lua 5.1**, per the manual's introduction. No JavaScript engine of any kind.
- Connection: `connectToServer(host, port, [save])`, `getConnectionInfo()` (4.2+; reports connected state from 4.12), `disconnect()`. Protocols negotiated: GMCP, MSDP, MSSP, MXP, MNES, CHARSET, MSP, MTTS, NAWS, NEW-ENVIRON, TLS, OSC 8 hyperlinks; ATCP as a legacy backup per the forum.
- GMCP: inbound packages land in the global `gmcp` table (`gmcp.Char.Vitals`); scripts bind event handlers to package names; outbound via `sendGMCP("Package.Name <json>")`, asynchronous.
- HTTP: `getHTTP(url, headers)` (4.10+), with `postHTTP`, `putHTTP`, `deleteHTTP`, `customHTTP` in the same family; results arrive as `sysGetHttpDone`/`sysGetHttpError` events; the event carries the body "if the body is under 10k characters".
- WebSocket: **no native function found**. The 2022 forum "Howto: Websockets in Mudlet" launches `websocat` through `spawn()` and reports trouble getting `send()` to work; the author fell back to HTTP.
- UI: **Geyser** layout manager: `Geyser.Container` (invisible layout; percent and negative offsets relative to the parent), `Geyser.Label` (rich-text subset, `rawEcho`, `createRightClickMenu`, tooltips), `Geyser.MiniConsole` (`display`), userwindows that may hold labels, miniconsoles and the mapper; the 4.20.1 public test builds (May 2026) add an **embeddable `Geyser.TextEdit`**, `selectAll` with window support, `getKeyCode`, an lpeg library and memory introspection. Releases 4.21.0/4.21.1 are listed; their notes were not reached.
- Local libraries: LuaSQL with SQLite, LuaFileSystem, lua-yajl (JSON), lua-zip, Luarocks, Discord integration.

**Reading for the lararium.**

1. **Tier (c), firmly.** Nothing in Mudlet can execute the ENGINE (Lua 5.1, no JS). Fengari runs Lua inside JS, not JS inside Lua [reached: search], so it does not help here. Mudlet is a projection, and a rich one.
2. **The port is GMCP, and the lararium poses as the game.** The lararium's node island opens a telnet listener on localhost (or the LAN Pi) and negotiates GMCP; Mudlet dials it with `connectToServer`. Package design, mirroring the Tiddler Port one to one *(inferred design)*:
   - `Lar.Open {tier:"glass", since, actor, renders:["text","label-html"], maxBytes}` ← Mudlet, at `sysConnectionEvent`.
   - `Lar.Snapshot {cursor, records[], membership[]}` and `Lar.Changes {cursor, prevCursor, records[], causedBy[]}` → Mudlet; records carry whole tiddlers, skinny by default (titles and fields; bodies on `Lar.Load`).
   - `Lar.Write {txId, basis, records[]}` ← Mudlet from a Geyser.TextEdit save or a label click; `Lar.Written {txId, status}` → Mudlet.
   - `Lar.Render {askId, title, target:"text"|"label-html"}` ← and `Lar.Answer` →, because Lua cannot run wikitext; the engine's fakedom camera renders to Mudlet's label-HTML subset *(inferred: the subset needs measuring)*.
   - `Lar.Presence` as an uncursored lane.
   GMCP payloads ride telnet subnegotiation as JSON (lua-yajl decodes); GMCP size limits are per-server, so chunk at a few KB *(inferred)*.
3. **Projection surfaces.** A `Geyser.Container` per open tiddler; a `Geyser.Label` for the rendered body (links via `createRightClickMenu` or `echoLink`); a `Geyser.MiniConsole` as the river log; `Geyser.TextEdit` as the editor (new in 4.20.1 test builds; on older Mudlet, fall back to an `echoLink` that opens the body in the command line or a userwindow); the mapper can project a `$:/tags/Map`-like structure if one exists *(inferred)*. Mudlet's own triggers and aliases let the player script against the wiki: `#tag foo` as an alias that sends `Lar.Write`.
4. **Running beside it.** Mudlet has no process host for Node, but a lararium beside it can be anything from §3.2: a Node island on the same machine, or the house's node vessel on a Pi. Mudlet's HTTP functions give a one-shot fallback for `Lar.Load` of a large body (under the ~10k event cap, else `downloadFile`).
5. **Law check.** Telnet and GMCP predate web2; they are a wire, not an ontology. The bridge sits in the node island behind a causal-island boundary; no Mudlet model enters the store. The Web3-only law is kept because the store never learns Mudlet exists.
6. **Charm.** This is the cheapest "tightly coupled" host in the whole list: one Lua package (a `.mpackage`), one GMCP listener in the island, and the operator's own MUD client becomes a wiki glass with triggers.

---

## 6. Prior art for one engine, many projections

- **ink (inkle).** C# runtime with an official Unity plugin; ports: inkjs (JS, "usually one major version behind"), inkcpp (C++ with a JSON-to-binary compiler, "optional Unreal and STL extensions"), Inkpot (The Chinese Room's UE 5.3+ plugin with InkPlusPlus kept close to the C# original "to ease updates"), godot-ink (C#), inkgd (GDScript, slower), ink_dart ("checked against the C# reference so that the same story and seed produce the same output and saves"), plus Java, Rust, Kotlin, Haxe, Lua (Defold, Love2d, Playdate) [reached: search]. Lesson: a reference runtime, a conformance oracle (same story + seed → same output), and ports that promise the oracle rather than the API. The engine-host conformance suite of v2 Phase 3 is this oracle for TW5.
- **Yarn Spinner.** "A generic dialogue virtual machine and script compiler written in C#, with nothing Unity-specific in it"; 2026: every Unity sample ported to a GDScript-only Godot version, a native Unreal integration being built "rather than a straight Unity port" [reached: search]. Lesson: the port hands the host *lines, options, commands*; the host draws. That is the `blocks` render target.
- **Twine story formats.** A format "supplies a visual layout, a set of macros, and internal JavaScript functionality"; the compiler combines passages with a format to produce a playable file; Snowman bundles Underscore, Marked and jQuery [reached: search]. Lesson, by contrast: the format fuses engine and projection, which is why Twine stories do not port off the browser. The three-layer frame avoids that fusion.
- **Fengari.** Lua 5.3 re-implemented in JS, DOM interop through the JS GC, `js.global` [reached: search]. Lesson: language VMs port *into* JS easily; the reverse (JS into Lua hosts) does not exist, so Lua hosts are projections.
- **Colyseus 0.18.** Server runs the authoritative simulation at a fixed tick; the client runs *the same step function* for prediction; "both need to be deterministic"; Babylon's Null Engine as a headless server, with code-duplication complaints [reached: search]. Lesson: the house's leader-and-replica topology (v2 P15) already matches; the engine is the shared step function, the store is the authoritative state, and projections predict.
- **Headless CMS.** Content and presentation decoupled; JSON over REST/GraphQL; "a channel-neutral model, organized around types of content and how they relate", versus page-oriented models that bind to one channel [reached: search]. Lesson: the tiddler is already channel-neutral; `$:/state/*` and story lists are the page-oriented residue the tier filter keeps on the glass (v2 P10).
- **MCP servers over notes.** Community Obsidian MCP servers expose notes as URI-addressed resources (`vault://note/…`, `obsidian://…`, `text/markdown`) with size and mtime metadata "so clients can validate caches", plus tools for search, links and writes; typical architecture is a stdio server bridging to an in-app HTTP server or the Vault API [reached: search]. The MCP spec: `resources/list`, `read`, `templates/list` (RFC 6570), `subscribe`, `notifications/resources/updated`, `list_changed`, text or base64 blob, annotations with `lastModified` [reached: fetched]. Lesson: MCP already is a Tiddler Port with worse cursors; see §9.
- **Foundry's common document model.** One ESM "common" module shared by Node server and browser client, "without a build or transpile step" [reached: search]. Lesson: the same code on both shores of a socket is normal in this ecosystem.

---

## 7. Mu

Tools per the seed: Wand `*` (visible feed, ignition), Cup `?` (macro, relation), Sword `!` (micro, discernment), Pentacle `_` (hidden feed, ground), Arcana `0` (unask).

**Wand `*` — what ignites.** Two fires in the visible feed. First: every non-JS host in the list (Mudlet, Verse, Discord, MCP, Bevy) can be a projection *tomorrow* with one addition to the port, a render ask with a declared target; without it they can only show titles. Second: the WASM line cuts straight through the embedded-engine family: Hermes and QuickJS carry the engine and drop the store. The fire reads: **the engine ports further than the store, and the store ports further than the DOM; design the port so each layer can stop where it must.**

**Cup `?` — the macro relation.** The field relates to a wiki the way it relates to a dialogue runtime: a reference VM in one language, conformance by oracle, hosts that draw lines and options. Browsers, Node, VS Code, Obsidian and Foundry all hold the same picture: a trusted process with the truth, a sandboxed surface over a JSON channel, the surface allowed to lag. Seen from the Cup, the house's node island is already the sidecar every game engine and text client wants; the work is adapters, not architecture.

**Sword `!` — the seams to cut.**
1. *JS-capable vs not* → engine inside vs projection over a port. (Mudlet, Verse, TTS, Roll20, Bevy fall right; everything else left.)
2. *WASM-capable vs not* → store inside vs store on a neighbor or lent natively. (Hermes, QuickJS, Extism fall right.)
3. *Durable vs evictable storage* → lararium vs replica that must sync before it may claim the name. (Tauri/Capacitor WebViews, Cloudflare DO after hibernation, Owlbear's 16 kB fall right until an adapter proves otherwise.)
4. *Timer-bearing vs pumped* → live wiki vs pure function. (Extism falls right.)
What must not be cut: one port for all projections. Ten adapters over one port stay cheap; ten ports become ten syncers.

**Pentacle `_` — the hidden ground.**
- Timers. TW5's refresh, the HandGate and the CoalesceGate all assume `setTimeout`; a pumped engine has never been run. Presence acknowledged, cost unclaimed.
- Memory on the small hosts: TidGi-Mobile's 4–10 s boots (v2 §0.10) say phones strain; QuickJS-in-UE and GodotJS have no published TW5 numbers at all.
- Verse. Everything known is keynote-grade; the transactional claim came through a forum quotation of Epic's post.
- CEF 59 in Unreal's stock browser widget: a DOM glass inside UE may be too old for the stock TW5 core *(inferred)*.
- Mudlet's label-HTML subset and GMCP size tolerance: unmeasured.
- Whether `new Function` over a 2.2 MB core is acceptable on Hermes (bytecode-first) or merely slow.

**Arcana `0` — unask the question.** "Which hosts can run TiddlyWiki?" Unasked: **which hosts can hold a port, and which can hold a store.** A host that holds only a port is a glass; a host that holds a port and a store but no engine is a cache; a host that holds all three is a lararium. The seed's shrine tiers already name this: a Household figure, a Crossroads node, a Temple. Hosts do not need to be lararia to be at the hearth.

**Carries.** *Overview `_?`*: the island doctrine ("as of my last sync") and the field's "replica may lag" are one drawing, and the durability column is where that drawing meets the ground. *Precision `_!`*: the first green for any new host is one test: open a port, receive a skinny snapshot, render one title to the host's native text, write one field back with a `txId`, see its echo named. Mudlet can pass that in a weekend.

---

## 8. Tier stories

**Gaia 2026 — Household: a phone and a Pi.** The Pi runs the node island (tier **a**: Node `worker_thread` engine, `storage-nodefs`, WebSocket server adapter); it holds the Web Lock of the household. The phone opens the hearth as a PWA in the mobile browser (tier **a**: engine in a Worker, Automerge over IndexedDB, stock TW5 on the glass over a loopback port). On the walk home the phone writes offline; on Wi-Fi the WebSocket adapter reconnects with `since: cursor` and the Pi merges. The phone's IndexedDB may be evicted by iOS one day; the Pi's disk is the durable island, so the phone's loss is a cache loss, named as such. If the household later wraps the hearth in Capacitor, the storage adapter moves to the native Filesystem plugin and nothing above it changes.

**Gaia 2026 — Crossroads: a Foundry VTT table.** The game's Node server (v13 "dedicated Node.js build") hosts a node island beside Foundry (tier **a**). A Foundry module, plain ESM shipped per the manifest, boots an engine replica in each player's browser (tier **a** per client) and syncs the store over an Automerge WebSocket adapter to the island; the GM's journal entries project from tiddlers through `port:ask{render:"html"}` into ApplicationV2 windows, and a token's context menu writes `port:write` with the player as actor. Foundry's own LevelDB documents are never the store; the module's socket channel carries presence only. Players on phones run the same module skinny.

**Gaia 2026 — Temple: a city-scale server.** A Node cluster behind a load balancer holds many islands, one per bag family, `storage-nodefs` on replicated disks, WebSocket server adapters per island (tier **a**). A Cloudflare Durable Object per public bag acts as a relay-only sync point for households that cannot reach the Temple directly; it holds Automerge bytes in its SQLite and forwards sync messages over hibernating WebSockets, but runs no engine and asserts no truth; it sits behind a causal-island boundary, as the Web3-only law asks of any web2 cloud. An MCP server at the Temple exposes public bags as resources so civic LLM harnesses can read and propose writes (tier **a** process, harness at **c**).

**Elyncia 5492 — single orichalcum figure.** A household Lar bound in one figure: a QuickJS-class core in the orichalcum (tier **b**), the engine inside, the store lent natively by the figure's own memory-lattice through host functions, no WASM anywhere in it. Its projection is the figure's face and voice (a `text` render) and the family's slate when one is brought near (a DOM glass over a short-range port). Fed daily, it hums; neglected, its cursor falls behind the district's and it says so by name.

**Elyncia 5492 — district node.** A Crossroads shrine runs a civic simulation in a UE6-like world: Verse logic on its single transactional thread, the lararium as a sidecar process on the same node (tier **c** for the world, **a** for the sidecar). Shrine panels, notice boards and the keeper's book are Scene Graph entities fed by `blocks` renders over the port; a citizen's offering at the board becomes a `port:write` with their persona key. The district's Mudlet-like terminal clients (the old text consoles of the Second Breaking) dial the same sidecar over GMCP and see the same records in Geyser panes.

**Elyncia 5492 — civic ley-line node.** The Temple tier: many engines on many threads, store on durable ley-line storage, relays at the ring road that hold bytes and no truth, an oracle-port where visiting spirits (LLM harnesses) read resources and propose writes through tools, each answered by name and ranked by the CRDT, never by the relay.

---

## 9. Ranked forward attractors, contract sketch, risks, what the branch gives

### 9.1 Ranked

| Rank | Host | Make it | Why now |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Browsers, PWAs, WebView shells (Capacitor/Tauri/Electron), WebXR | **first-class (a)** | already the plan of record; add the native-FS storage adapter for shells because WebView storage is evictable |
| 2 | Node / Deno / Bun, Pi, Termux | **first-class (a)** | the node island exists; fix survey gap #10; add Deno's module-worker and permission shape to the conformance suite |
| 3 | VS Code extension, Obsidian plugin | **first-class (a)** | their extension-host/webview split is the port drawn by someone else; one adapter each |
| 4 | MCP server | **first-class projection** | resources ↔ membership+records, `subscribe`/`updated` ↔ `port:changes` without payload, tools ↔ `port:write`/`port:ask`; the only new work is a cursor in resource annotations |
| 5 | Foundry VTT module | **(a) both ends** | Crossroads tier in one package; browser plus Node, both already supported |
| 6 | Mudlet over GMCP | **projection-only (c)** | cheapest tightly-coupled non-JS host; a weekend package; proves the render ask |
| 7 | Owlbear Rodeo | **(b)/(c)** | an iframe is a browser; 16 kB limits keep OBR as projection |
| 8 | UE6 sidecar (and UE5 via PuerTS) | **(c), watch for (b)** | nothing to build until Verse or CEF moves; UE5 PuerTS is a bounded experiment |
| 9 | Neovim, Emacs, terminal TUI, Discord/Matrix bots | **projection-only, community** | all four are a Node process plus a text projection |
| 10 | Godot (GodotJS), Unity (PuerTS/Jint) | **(b) when asked** | engine-inside works; store needs host-lent Automerge or a neighbor |
| 11 | Cloudflare Durable Objects, smart TVs (Tizen 8+) | **relay-only / lean (a)** | DO holds bytes, never truth; TVs are browsers of their year |
| drop | Roll20, e-readers, ESP32-class, Bevy | **(d)** (static camera or leaf at most) | sandbox without HTTP; basic JS only; heap; no JS |

### 9.2 The minimal engine-host contract (interface sketch)

```ts
// What a host lends. Everything optional is a capability; the engine refuses by name without it.
interface HostCapabilities {
  language: "es2020" | "es2023";          // proven by the conformance suite, never detected
  evaluate(src: string, params: string[]): Function;   // new Function or an equivalent
  schedule?: (ms: number, fn: () => void) => () => void; // absent => engine runs in pump mode
  now(): number;                           // monotonic ms
  channel: MessageChannelLike;             // postMessage(json) / onmessage; JSON-serializable only
  wasm?: boolean;                          // true => Automerge-in-JS may load
  storage?: StorageAdapterLike & { durable: boolean; budgetBytes?: number };
  network?: NetworkAdapterLike[];          // websocket | messagechannel | stdio | none
  threads?: { spawn(src: string): WorkerLike };
  memoryBudgetBytes?: number;              // below ~16 MB the host is a leaf, not an engine host
}

// Pump mode for hosts without timers (Extism-class, some game-engine ticks).
interface PumpedEngine { tick(nowMs: number): void; }

// The port, as v2 §5 plus the three refinements of §2.3 above.
type ProjectionOpen = {
  portId: string; wikiUri: string; tier: "glass" | "headless";
  since?: Cursor; actor: { personaUri?: string; deviceKey: string };
  localFilter: string; skinny?: boolean;
  renders: Array<"html" | "text" | "blocks" | "label-html">;   // what this projection can show
  edits: boolean; presence: boolean;
  maxRecordBytes?: number; maxBatchBytes?: number;             // Owlbear 16 KB, Discord 2 000 chars, GMCP chunks
};
type Ask =
  | { kind: "filter"; filter: string }
  | { kind: "render"; title: string; target: "html" | "text" | "blocks" | "label-html" }
  | { kind: "verb"; verb: string; args: unknown }
  | { kind: "load"; title: string }
  | { kind: "cas"; cid: string }
  | { kind: "invoke"; renderCursor: Cursor; affordanceId: string; arg?: unknown }; // grain 2, §2.4
// A "blocks" render answers { renderCursor, blocks[], affordances: Array<{ id, kind: "link"|"button"|"toggle"|"select"|"edit"|"drop", label, target?, field? }> }.
// Acts from any projection arrive only as port:write{txId, basis, records}, port:ask{kind:"verb"}, or port:ask{kind:"invoke"} (capability-gated, stale by cursor).
```

Three rules ride with it: (1) a projection declares its renders and limits at open; (2) the engine never emits an event, only records, answers and faults; (3) a host that lends no durable storage is a replica and the UI says so.

### 9.3 Risks

- **The WASM split.** Automerge-in-JS does not run on Hermes or QuickJS; native Automerge through host functions is unbuilt in this house. Until it exists, phones in React Native shells and QuickJS game embeds are tier (b) only. Mitigation: keep the store behind the storage/network adapter seam so a native adapter can slot in; measure a Node-backend PuerTS first because it sidesteps the split.
- **Timers.** Pump mode is a design, not a run. Risk: TW5's own throttles misbehave under a host tick. Mitigation: make `schedule` the first conformance case.
- **Storage eviction in shells.** Tauri (per PowerSync) and iOS WebViews can lose IndexedDB/OPFS. Mitigation: shells ship the native-FS adapter from day one; the UI names non-durable hosts.
- **Verse unknowns.** Everything is keynote-grade until "The Road to UE 6" and later docs are read directly. Mitigation: no UE6 work before early access; UE5 PuerTS as the bounded probe.
- **Port proliferation.** Each host tempts a bespoke protocol (GMCP packages, MCP resources, OBR broadcasts). Mitigation: one port, adapters only; any adapter that needs a new kind proposes it to the port, not to its host.
- **Web2 leakage.** Cloudflare, Discord and Foundry's socket.io are web2 systems. Mitigation: they carry bytes or projections only, behind a causal-island boundary; the store never models them (seed, Config Defaults).
- **Old browsers inside engines.** UE's CEF 59 and webOS's Chromium 68–87 may not run the stock 5.4.0 core. Mitigation: those hosts take `text`/`blocks` projections, not a glass.

### 9.4 What the current branch already gives (per the survey, not re-read)

| Toward | Branch piece | Survey ref |
|---|---|---|
| Engine without DOM or Node | third-runtime forge: `$tw.browser = $tw.node = null`, `configureIslandRuntime`, non-exiting `utils.error` (browser path) | §3.1 |
| Host capability injection | `tw5-host-bridge.ts`: `require` allowlist, `fs` denying Proxy, `window` Proxy admitting only `sjcl` and `$tw` | §3.1 |
| Integrity across hosts | kernel pulls core and plugins by CID and re-hashes them | §3.1 |
| Store abstraction | Automerge doc per bag; island Repo ⇄ vessel Repo over MessageChannel; `LarWSClientAdapter` over WebSocket; IndexedDB Repo in the browser vessel | §2, §3.2 |
| Node host | `worker_thread` path, disk projector, WS relay (CRDT sync only, never DOM) | §2, §3.1 |
| Neutral render for non-DOM projections | fakedom camera renders RootTemplate and PageStylesheet to HTML in the worker (`tw5-projection.ts`, `tw5-camera.ts`) | §3.4 |
| Port envelopes | `island-protocol.ts` envelope family, door guard, verb table, `wiki:place-verb`/`verb-result` | §3.3 |
| Echo and pacing discipline | LandedLedger foam guard, HandGate 400 ms / 2 s | §3.2 |
| Multi-tab leadership | origin Web Lock (`vessel-lock.ts`) | §3.6 |
| **Missing** | a generic `filter`/`render` ask on the live island (only orphaned `meme-worker-script.ts`); Node path asymmetries (#10); `$tw.version` undefined on node; trusted-module gate mis-wired (#11) | §3.3, §4 |

The single most leveraged next step for the whole host field is the one v2 already scheduled for Phase 1: **`port:ask` with `filter`, `render` and `load`**, because every tier-(c) host in this report needs exactly that and nothing else from the engine.

